::ENG3_U02_L06_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when city parks are being built in crowded industrial neighborhoods. Author A: A park is not a luxury. It is a lung for the city, giving workers clean air after hours beside smoke and machines. Author B: A park may offer air, but it cannot hide the smoke that made it necessary. We should not praise grass while ignoring the factories that darken it. What shared topic do both authors address? {
=The role of city parks in industrial neighborhoods.#Correct. Both authors address parks in the context of industrial cities.
~The best way to design machines.#Machines are context, not the main topic.
~The history of luxury goods.#Luxury appears only in Author A's opening claim.
~The color of grass after rain.#Grass is a detail, not the shared topic.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when city parks are being built in crowded industrial neighborhoods. Author A: A park is not a luxury. It is a lung for the city, giving workers clean air after hours beside smoke and machines. Author B: A park may offer air, but it cannot hide the smoke that made it necessary. We should not praise grass while ignoring the factories that darken it. How does Author A reason about parks? {
~Parks are harmful because they attract smoke.#Author A presents parks as helpful.
=Parks are necessary because they provide relief from industrial conditions.#Correct. Author A calls a park a city lung and emphasizes clean air for workers.
~Parks should replace all factories immediately.#Author A does not make this claim.
~Parks are useful only for wealthy citizens.#Author A connects parks to workers.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when city parks are being built in crowded industrial neighborhoods. Author A: A park is not a luxury. It is a lung for the city, giving workers clean air after hours beside smoke and machines. Author B: A park may offer air, but it cannot hide the smoke that made it necessary. We should not praise grass while ignoring the factories that darken it. How does Author B reason about parks? {
~Parks should be praised without question.#Author B questions praise that ignores factory smoke.
~Parks are only useful for machines.#This is unsupported.
=Parks may help, but they should not distract from industrial problems.#Correct. Author B acknowledges air but warns against ignoring factories.
~Parks are unrelated to industrial life.#Author B directly connects parks to industrial smoke.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when city parks are being built in crowded industrial neighborhoods. Author A: A park is not a luxury. It is a lung for the city, giving workers clean air after hours beside smoke and machines. Author B: A park may offer air, but it cannot hide the smoke that made it necessary. We should not praise grass while ignoring the factories that darken it. Which comparison best reflects their reasoning? {
~Both authors argue parks are useless.#Author A supports parks, and Author B acknowledges they may offer air.
~Author A rejects workers, while Author B supports factories.#This reverses and distorts the passages.
~Both authors focus only on park decoration.#Neither focuses mainly on decoration.
=Author A sees parks as relief, while Author B sees them as incomplete reform.#Correct. This comparison explains the difference in reasoning.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when city parks are being built in crowded industrial neighborhoods. Author A: A park is not a luxury. It is a lung for the city, giving workers clean air after hours beside smoke and machines. Author B: A park may offer air, but it cannot hide the smoke that made it necessary. We should not praise grass while ignoring the factories that darken it. What context best explains both passages? {
~A period when farming tools are disappearing.#The passages focus on industrial neighborhoods, not farming tools.
~A period when cities are emptying completely.#The context says neighborhoods are crowded.
=A period when industrial city life raises concerns about workers' health and public space.#Correct. Both authors respond to industrial conditions and urban reform.
~A period when literature no longer discusses public issues.#The passages clearly discuss public issues.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public schools are expanding. Author A: A schoolhouse is a promise that talent need not be born wealthy. Author B: A schoolhouse is a promise only if every child can enter it safely and remain there long enough to learn. What shared topic do both authors address? {
~The design of wealthy homes.#Wealth is mentioned, but the topic is schooling.
~The safest path to a building.#Safety appears as part of access, not the whole topic.
~The cost of school supplies.#Supplies are not discussed.
=The meaning and access of public schooling.#Correct. Both authors address what school expansion should mean.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public schools are expanding. Author A: A schoolhouse is a promise that talent need not be born wealthy. Author B: A schoolhouse is a promise only if every child can enter it safely and remain there long enough to learn. How do the authors differ? {
=Author A emphasizes opportunity, while Author B emphasizes conditions needed for real access.#Correct. Author A praises possibility; Author B adds requirements for access.
~Author A rejects education, while Author B rejects children.#Both value education.
~Author A discusses safety, while Author B discusses wealth only.#This reverses the focus.
~Both authors argue schools are unnecessary.#Neither argues that.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public schools are expanding. Author A: A schoolhouse is a promise that talent need not be born wealthy. Author B: A schoolhouse is a promise only if every child can enter it safely and remain there long enough to learn. Which statement best paraphrases Author B? {
~Schools should be closed because children are unsafe.#Author B does not call for closing schools.
~Only wealthy children have talent.#That idea is closer to what Author A challenges.
=Schools matter only when access and continued attendance are possible.#Correct. Author B says the promise depends on safe entry and enough time to learn.
~School buildings are more important than learning.#Author B focuses on meaningful access to learning.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public schools are expanding. Author A: A schoolhouse is a promise that talent need not be born wealthy. Author B: A schoolhouse is a promise only if every child can enter it safely and remain there long enough to learn. Which detail from Author B most directly supports the issue of access? {
~A schoolhouse is a promise.#This repeats the shared idea but not the access condition.
=Every child can enter it safely.#Correct. This phrase directly addresses access.
~Talent need not be born wealthy.#This belongs to Author A.
~Born wealthy.#This is not Author B's detail.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public schools are expanding. Author A: A schoolhouse is a promise that talent need not be born wealthy. Author B: A schoolhouse is a promise only if every child can enter it safely and remain there long enough to learn. Which comparison avoids a common mistake? {
~Both authors use the word promise, so they have identical reasoning.#Shared wording does not mean identical reasoning.
~The passages differ only because Author B writes a longer sentence.#Length is not a meaningful reasoning comparison.
~Author A is about wealth, so Author B is unrelated.#Both address public schooling.
=Both authors value schooling, but Author A stresses opportunity while Author B stresses meaningful access.#Correct. This comparison addresses both similarity and difference.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a period when inexpensive cameras are becoming common. Author A: A camera lets ordinary families keep proof of their own joys. Author B: A camera can preserve joy, but it can also teach people to perform happiness for others. What shared topic is addressed? {
=The effect of common cameras on ordinary life.#Correct. Both authors discuss what cameras do in people's lives.
~The price of theater tickets.#Performance is figurative here, not theater tickets.
~The invention of paper maps.#Maps are not mentioned.
~The decline of family life only.#Author A emphasizes preserving family joy.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a period when inexpensive cameras are becoming common. Author A: A camera lets ordinary families keep proof of their own joys. Author B: A camera can preserve joy, but it can also teach people to perform happiness for others. Which comparison is most accurate? {
~Both authors believe cameras are only harmful.#Author A is positive, and Author B is mixed.
~Author A discusses wealth, while Author B discusses travel.#Neither focuses on travel.
=Author A emphasizes memory, while Author B questions how cameras shape behavior.#Correct. Author A values preserving joy; Author B worries about performed happiness.
~Both authors focus on camera repair.#Repair is not discussed.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a period when inexpensive cameras are becoming common. Author A: A camera lets ordinary families keep proof of their own joys. Author B: A camera can preserve joy, but it can also teach people to perform happiness for others. What does Author B acknowledge before raising concern? {
~Cameras are too expensive for families.#The context says inexpensive cameras are becoming common.
=Cameras can preserve joy.#Correct. Author B concedes a benefit before questioning its effect.
~Cameras should be banned.#Author B does not say this.
~Families never feel joy.#Author B says joy can be preserved.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a period when inexpensive cameras are becoming common. Author A: A camera lets ordinary families keep proof of their own joys. Author B: A camera can preserve joy, but it can also teach people to perform happiness for others. What time-period issue connects both passages? {
~Whether painting should replace photography.#Painting is not discussed.
~Whether families should stop gathering.#The passages do not argue that.
~Whether ordinary people should avoid technology completely.#Neither author argues complete avoidance.
=How new technology changes personal memory and public self-presentation.#Correct. Both authors address the social meaning of common cameras.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a period when inexpensive cameras are becoming common. Author A: A camera lets ordinary families keep proof of their own joys. Author B: A camera can preserve joy, but it can also teach people to perform happiness for others. Which answer overstates the evidence? {
~Author A sees cameras as useful for family memory.#This is supported by "keep proof of their own joys."
~Author B thinks cameras can influence behavior.#This is supported by "perform happiness."
=Author B proves cameras destroy every honest emotion.#Correct. This is too absolute and goes beyond the passage.
~Both authors discuss ordinary people's use of cameras.#This is supported by the context and passages.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when postal delivery is expanding. Author A: A letter can shorten distance without moving a body. Author B: A letter can travel quickly, but it cannot mend every absence it describes. What shared topic do both authors address? {
~The construction of roads.#Roads are not mentioned.
=The emotional and social effect of expanded letter delivery.#Correct. Both authors address how letters affect distance and absence.
~The cost of moving houses.#Moving is figurative in Author A's sentence.
~The best style of handwriting.#Handwriting is not discussed.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when postal delivery is expanding. Author A: A letter can shorten distance without moving a body. Author B: A letter can travel quickly, but it cannot mend every absence it describes. How does Author A reason about letters? {
~Letters make absence worse.#That is closer to Author B's concern.
~Letters are useless because bodies do not move.#Author A sees letters as reducing distance.
~Letters should replace every visit.#Author A does not go that far.
=Letters help people feel connected across physical distance.#Correct. "Shorten distance" supports this reasoning.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when postal delivery is expanding. Author A: A letter can shorten distance without moving a body. Author B: A letter can travel quickly, but it cannot mend every absence it describes. How does Author B's reasoning differ? {
~Author B thinks letters cannot physically travel.#Author B says a letter can travel quickly.
=Author B warns that communication cannot fully solve separation.#Correct. The letter cannot mend every absence.
~Author B focuses on road safety.#Road safety is not discussed.
~Author B denies that absence exists.#Author B directly mentions absence.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when postal delivery is expanding. Author A: A letter can shorten distance without moving a body. Author B: A letter can travel quickly, but it cannot mend every absence it describes. Which comparison is strongest? {
~Both authors argue letters are dangerous.#Neither passage says that.
~Author A focuses on absence, while Author B focuses only on speed.#This is incomplete and partly reversed.
=Author A emphasizes connection, while Author B emphasizes the limits of written contact.#Correct. This captures both positions.
~Both authors say bodies should never move.#This misreads Author A's figurative wording.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when postal delivery is expanding. Author A: A letter can shorten distance without moving a body. Author B: A letter can travel quickly, but it cannot mend every absence it describes. Which evidence from Author B supports a more cautious view? {
~A letter can travel quickly.#This acknowledges a benefit, not the caution.
~Postal delivery is expanding.#This is context, not Author B's evidence.
~Without moving a body.#This belongs to Author A.
=It cannot mend every absence.#Correct. This phrase shows the limitation Author B emphasizes.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a period when public libraries are opening in towns. Author A: A library places a hundred teachers on a single street. Author B: A library opens a door, but a tired worker may still have no evening left to walk through it. What shared topic do both authors address? {
=The value and limits of public library access.#Correct. Both authors discuss libraries and access.
~The design of school desks.#Schools are not the shared topic.
~The number of teachers in one building.#Author A's phrase is figurative.
~The history of street paving.#The street detail is not the topic.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a period when public libraries are opening in towns. Author A: A library places a hundred teachers on a single street. Author B: A library opens a door, but a tired worker may still have no evening left to walk through it. How does Author B complicate Author A's optimism? {
~Author B says libraries contain no books.#This is unsupported.
~Author B argues teachers are harmful.#Author B does not discuss teachers directly.
=Author B suggests access requires time and energy, not just an open building.#Correct. The tired worker may have no evening left.
~Author B says streets should be closed.#The street is not Author B's focus.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a period when public libraries are opening in towns. Author A: A library places a hundred teachers on a single street. Author B: A library opens a door, but a tired worker may still have no evening left to walk through it. Which phrase from Author A shows positive reasoning? {
~A tired worker.#This phrase appears in Author B.
=A hundred teachers.#Correct. This metaphor presents the library as a source of learning.
~No evening left.#This phrase supports Author B's concern.
~Walk through it.#This is part of Author B's access image.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a period when public libraries are opening in towns. Author A: A library places a hundred teachers on a single street. Author B: A library opens a door, but a tired worker may still have no evening left to walk through it. What context best explains both passages? {
~Towns are banning reading completely.#The context says libraries are opening.
~Workers are no longer tired.#Author B says the worker is tired.
~Public libraries have no connection to education.#Author A directly connects libraries to teachers.
=Public learning opportunities are expanding, but practical barriers may remain.#Correct. Both authors respond to access and its limits.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a period when public libraries are opening in towns. Author A: A library places a hundred teachers on a single street. Author B: A library opens a door, but a tired worker may still have no evening left to walk through it. Which comparison best meets the standard? {
~Author A writes first, and Author B writes second.#Order is not a meaningful comparison of reasoning.
~Both authors use the word library, so their ideas are identical.#Shared topic does not prove identical reasoning.
=Both authors address library access, but Author A emphasizes educational opportunity while Author B emphasizes practical limits on using it.#Correct. This compares topic, reasoning, and context.
~Author B is wrong because Author A is more positive.#Evaluation requires reasoning, not preference.
}
